WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.460
Ever bumped into a glass door?

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.560
It seems like bats do it pretty often,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.920
and not because they’re texting.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.000
A new study shows that bats fail to recognize

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.400
human-made vertical, smooth surfaces,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.400
like glass windows, as obstacles.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.880
The researchers placed a vertical metal plate

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.860
in the corner of a flight tunnel.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:24.770
Then, they monitored bats as they flew through it.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:29.200
Of 21 bats, 19 crashed at least once into the plate,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.080
but never into walls or other objects

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.540
Collisions also occurred when the researchers

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.900
placed the metal plates outside of caves of

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:39.520
three different bat species.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.520
This is because smooth surfaces limit bats’ ability

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.420
to navigate through the dark using

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.000
their echolocation system.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.480
Bats emit high-frequency sounds

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.100
and use the returning echoes

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.640
to spot obstacles in their surroundings.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.940
But a sleek, vertical surface approached at an angle

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.700
reflects away the echoes,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.300
fooling bats into recognizing them as open flyways.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.460
The scientists now hope to determine if

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:10.680
windows or glass buildings pose an ecological threat

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.340
to bats and how these animals

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.560
can learn to deal with them.

